    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.]\n\nContent:\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n[MATH: \\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nWe usually let [MATH: u = x,] but that would leave us with [MATH: v' = \\ln(x)] which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our [MATH: u] and [MATH: v'] slightly differently, as follows:\n[MATH: u = \\ln x|\u27f9u^{\u2032} = (1)/(x); v^{\u2032} = x|\u27f9v = (1)/(2)x^{2}.]\n\nContent:\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222bx\\ln xdx|= \u222b\\ln x_(\u23df)_(u) \u22c5 x_(\u23df)_(v^{\u2032})dx; = \\ln x \u22c5 (1)/(2)x^{2} \u2212 \u222b(1)/(2)x^{2} \u22c5 (1)/(x)dx; = (1)/(2)x^{2}\\ln x \u2212 (1)/(2)\u222bxdx; = (1)/(2)x^{2}\\ln x \u2212 (1)/(4)x^{2} + C.]\n\nContent:\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should always identify the logarithmic function with [MATH: u,] not with [MATH: v'.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e} 4x\\ln\\left(x^3\\right) \\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining [MATH: u] and [MATH: v',] as follows:\n\n[MATH: u = \\ln (x^{3})|\u27f9u^{\u2032} = (1)/(x^{3}) \u22c5 3x^{2} = (3)/(x); v^{\u2032} = 4x|\u27f9v = \u222b4xdx = 2x^{2}]\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{e}4x\\ln (x^{3})dx|= \u222b_{1}^{e}uv^{\u2032}dx; = uv|_{1}^{e} \u2212 \u222b_{1}^{e}vu^{\u2032}dx; = \\ln (x^{3}) \u22c5 (2x^{2})|_{1}^{e} \u2212 \u222b_{1}^{e}(2x^{2}) \u22c5 (3)/(x)dx; = [2(e)^{2}\\ln (e^{3}) \u2212 2(1)^{2}\\ln (1^{3})] \u2212 6\u222b_{1}^{e}xdx; = 6e^{2} \u2212 6((1)/(2)x^{2})|_{1}^{e}; = 6e^{2} \u2212 3x^{2}|_{1}^{e}; = 6e^{2} \u2212 3(e^{2} \u2212 1^{2}); = 3e^{2} + 3.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b12x^{5}\\ln xdx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (x^{6})/(3) \u2212 (x^{6}\\ln x)/(6) + C]\nb. [MATH: (x^{6})/(36) \u2212 (x^{6}\\ln x)/(6) + C]\nc. [MATH: 2x^{6}\\ln x \u2212 (x^{6})/(3) + C]\nd. [MATH: 2x^{6}\\ln x \u2212 (x^{6})/(6) + C]\ne. [MATH: (x^{6}\\ln x)/(36) + (x^{6})/(6) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{e}x^{3}\\ln xdx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(16)(3e^{4} + 1)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(16)(e^{4} + 1)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(4)(3e^{4} \u2212 1)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(16)(4e^{4} \u2212 1)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(4)(3e^{4} + 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log_4{x}}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe define\n[MATH: u = \\log_{4} x|\u27f9u^{\u2032} = (1)/(x\\ln 4); v^{\u2032} = (1)/(x^{3})|\u27f9v = \u222b(1)/(x^{3})dx = \u2212 (1)/(2x^{2}).]\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n[MATH: \u222b(\\log_{4} x)/(x^{3})dx|= \u222buv^{\u2032}dx; = uv \u2212 \u222bvu^{\u2032}dx; = \\log_{4} x(\u2212 (1)/(2x^{2})) \u2212 \u222b(\u2212 (1)/(2x^{2}))(1)/(x\\ln 4)dx; = \u2212 (1)/(2x^{2})\\log_{4} x + (1)/(2\\ln 4)\u222b(1)/(x^{3})dx; = \u2212 (\\ln x)/(2x^{2}\\ln 4) + (1)/(2\\ln 4)(\u2212 (1)/(2x^{2})) + C; = \u2212 (\\ln x)/(2x^{2}\\ln 4) \u2212 (1)/(4x^{2}\\ln 4) + C; = \u2212 (2\\ln x + 1)/(4x^{2}\\ln 4) + C,]\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula [MATH: \\log_4{x}=\\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{\\ln{4}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222bx\\log_{2} (3x)dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (x^{2})/(2\\ln 2)(\\ln (3x) + 2) + C]\nb. [MATH: (x^{2})/(4\\ln 2)(2\\ln (3x) \u2212 1) + C]\nc. [MATH: (x^{2})/(2\\ln 2)(x\\ln (3x) \u2212 2) + C]\nd. [MATH: (x^{2})/(2\\ln 2)(\\ln (3x) \u2212 1) + C]\ne. [MATH: (x)/(4\\ln 2)(2\\ln (3x) + 1) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b(\\log_{3} x)/(x^{3})dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (\\ln x + 2)/(x^{2}\\ln 3) + C]\nb. [MATH: (2\\ln x + 1)/(x^{2}\\ln 3) + C]\nc. [MATH: (\\ln x + 2)/(4x^{2}\\ln 3) + C]\nd. [MATH: (\\ln x + 1)/(2x^{2}\\ln 3) + C]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (2\\ln x + 1)/(4x^{2}\\ln 3) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nAt first, it may not look like we have two factors. Actually, we do, but one of them is implicit. The trick is to write the integral as\n[MATH: \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\ln{x} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\textrm{d}x.]\n\nUsing this trick, we can now use integration by parts. We let\n\n[MATH: u = \\ln x|\u27f9u^{\u2032} = (1)/(x); v^{\u2032} = 1|\u27f9v = x.]\n\nNow, using the by-parts formula, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b\\ln xdx|= x\\ln x \u2212 \u222bx \u22c5 (1)/(x)dx; = x\\ln x \u2212 \u222b1dx; = x\\ln x \u2212 x + C; = x(\\ln x \u2212 1) + C.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b\\ln (x^{2})dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x\\ln (x^{2}) \u2212 x + C]\nb. [MATH: x^{2}\\ln (x^{2}) \u2212 2x + C]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(x) \u2212 2x + C]\nd. [MATH: x\\ln (x^{2}) \u2212 \\ln|x|+ C]\ne. [MATH: x\\ln (x^{2}) \u2212 2x + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \u222b_{1/5}^{e^{2}/5}\\ln (5x)dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(5)(e^{2} \u2212 1)]\nb. [MATH: e^{2} + 1]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2)(e^{5} + 1)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(5)(e^{2} + 1)]\ne. [MATH: 5(1 \u2212 e^{2})]"
  }
